BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:human-rights-watch-ai-strategy-solutions-lead-2026@artificialnouveau.gi
 thub.io
DTSTAMP:20261025T090229Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: Human Rights Watch: AI Strategy and Solutions Lead (one-y
 ear fixed term)
DESCRIPTION:Human Rights Watch is hiring an AI Strategy and Solutions Lead 
 into its Operations Department to build the foundation for responsible AI 
 adoption across the organisation. The role deliberately sits between strat
 egy and execution: assessing organisational readiness\, mapping partnershi
 ps and governance\, and also prototyping and shipping working systems. THE
  WORK\, in four strands. (1) STRATEGY AND READINESS: with the CIO and the 
 Information Security and Technology (IST) leadership team\, build an AI st
 rategy covering data foundation requirements\, infrastructure gaps and org
 anisational readiness\; assess current state and shape a roadmap balancing
  quick wins against long-term capacity\; research what peer nonprofit\, hu
 manitarian and human rights organisations are doing and find collaboration
  openings. (2) FLAGSHIP SOLUTIONS: work with departments to find high-impa
 ct use cases\, translate business needs into technical approaches\, build 
 and deploy AI/ML solutions with measurable value (time saved\, efficiency\
 , better outcomes)\, using the Microsoft AI ecosystem (Copilot Studio\, Az
 ure AI\, Power Platform) among other tools\, and oversee consultants and v
 endors to extend delivery capacity. (3) ADOPTION AND CAPACITY: balance onb
 oarding staff onto AI tools against building custom solutions where they a
 mplify the organisation's strengths\; write training material pitched at d
 ifferent work areas and skill levels covering prompting and responsible us
 e\; support working groups and communities of practice\; produce playbooks
  and guides for non-technical staff. (4) GOVERNANCE: partner with IST lead
 ership on risk assessment frameworks\, align solutions with security\, inf
 rastructure and architecture requirements\, contribute to AI policy develo
 pment and its operationalisation\, set standards for evaluating proofs of 
 concept and MVPs\, and engage business units so outcomes are co-owned\; al
 l of it balanced against data quality\, security and resource constraints 
 and HRW's mission as a human rights organisation. WHO THEY WANT: a bachelo
 r's degree or equivalent work experience in computer science\, data scienc
 e\, information systems\, mathematics\, AI/ML implementation or a related 
 technical field\; a minimum of seven years' relevant experience including 
 two to three years on AI and two to three years implementing AI/ML in prod
 uction\; experience contributing to technology strategy and assessing orga
 nisational readiness for new technology\; strong Python and ML library/fra
 mework skills\; hands-on predictive modelling\, data science and statistic
 al analysis\; experience with low-code/no-code AI platforms such as Copilo
 t Studio or Power Platform\; a track record of scoping and delivering unde
 r resource constraints\; a grasp of both generative AI and traditional ML\
 ; and the ability to explain technical concepts plainly to mixed audiences
 \, engage stakeholders and lead technology adoption as an internal partner
  to business teams. PREFERRED: nonprofit\, humanitarian or human rights ex
 perience\; data privacy\, security and ethical AI knowledge\; familiarity 
 with responsible AI frameworks and governance models\; vendor and consulta
 nt management\; and a background in politically sensitive or high-risk env
 ironments. APPLY: through the HRW job portal with a cover letter and CV\, 
 preferably as PDFs\, by 30 August 2026. Only complete applications are rev
 iewed and only shortlisted candidates are contacted\; no calls or email en
 quiries. HRW states that it does not discriminate on the basis of disabili
 ty\, age\, gender identity and expression\, national origin\, race and eth
 nicity\, religious beliefs\, sexual orientation or criminal record.\n\nOrg
 anisation: Human Rights Watch (HRW)\, Operations Department\nLocation: Mus
 t be within commuting distance of an HRW office: Amsterdam\, Amman\, Berli
 n\, Brussels\, London\, Stockholm\, New York\, Washington DC\, Miami\, Sao
  Paulo\, Toronto or Chicago. Reports to the Chief Information Officer in B
 erlin. No relocation support and no work-authorisation sponsorship.\nAward
 : USD 102\,000 to 112\,000 plus employer-paid benefits if based in the US\
 ; ranges outside the US are set by local market conditions. No application
  fee.\nDuration: One year fixed-term\, full time. Application deadline 30 
 August 2026.\n\nMore: https://job-boards.greenhouse.io/humanrightswatch/jo
 bs/8350654002?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://job-boards.greenhouse.io/humanrightswatch/jobs/8350654002?utm_s
 ource=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Must be within commuting distance of an HRW office: Amsterdam\, Am
 man\, Berlin\, Brussels\, London\, Stockholm\, New York\, Washington DC\, 
 Miami\, Sao Paulo\, Toronto or Chicago. Reports to the Chief Information O
 fficer in Berlin. No relocation support and no work-authorisation sponsors
 hip.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:acls-digital-justice-grants-2026-27@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261025T090229Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261103
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261104
SUMMARY:Deadline: ACLS Digital Justice Grants 2026-27 (Seed and Development
 )
DESCRIPTION:ACLS Digital Justice Grants fund digital projects in the humani
 ties and interpretive social sciences that critically engage with the inte
 rests and histories of people of colour and other historically marginalise
 d communities\, including Black\, Latinx\, Indigenous\, disabled and LGBTQ
 + communities. The programme's stated aims are to diversify the digital do
 main\, advance justice and equity in digital scholarly practice\, and cont
 ribute to public understanding of racial and social justice issues\, with 
 an explicit interest in scholars working with under-utilised or understudi
 ed source materials and those at institutions with less infrastructure for
  digital work. TWO TRACKS\, one deadline: SEED GRANTS of USD 10\,000 to 25
 \,000 for projects in the start-up or prototyping phase\, covering plannin
 g\, prototyping and methodology testing\; and DEVELOPMENT GRANTS of USD 50
 \,000 to 100\,000 for projects that have moved past proof of concept and c
 an show preliminary work and evidence of engagement. ELIGIBILITY: at least
  one principal investigator must be a scholar in the humanities or interpr
 etive social sciences\, and awarded funds must be administered by a US ins
 titution of higher education. WHAT IS NOT FUNDED: creative works such as n
 ovels or films\, textbooks\, straightforward translations\, purely pedagog
 ical projects\, and institutional indirect costs. Note that outputs are ex
 pected to be made as widely available as intellectual property allows\, id
 eally under the most liberal open-source or Creative Commons licence. TIMI
 NG: applications go through the ACLS online fellowship and grant administr
 ation system (ofa.acls.org) by 3 November 2026 at 17:00 ET\; grant terms b
 egin between 1 July and 31 December 2027 and run 12 to 18 months. The prog
 ramme is funded by the Mellon Foundation.\n\nOrganisation: American Counci
 l of Learned Societies (ACLS)\, funded by the Mellon Foundation\nLocation:
  Grant funds must be administered by a US institution of higher education.
  No citizenship or residency restriction is stated for the principal inves
 tigator.\nAward: Seed Grants USD 10\,000 to 25\,000 for start-up and proto
 typing work\; Development Grants USD 50\,000 to 100\,000 for projects past
  proof of concept. Institutional indirect costs are not covered. No applic
 ation fee.\nDuration: Grant terms run 12 to 18 months\, starting between 1
  July 2027 and 31 December 2027. Deadline 3 November 2026\, 17:00 ET.\n\nM
 ore: https://www.acls.org/programs/acls-digital-justice-grants/?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.acls.org/programs/acls-digital-justice-grants/?utm_source=a
 rtificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Grant funds must be administered by a US institution of higher edu
 cation. No citizenship or residency restriction is stated for the principa
 l investigator.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:titanik-mcult-residing-non-linear-technologies-sonic-worldings-2027@art
 ificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261025T090229Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260830
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260831
SUMMARY:Deadline: Residing Non-linear Technologies and Sonic Worldings (M-C
 ult x Titanik\, Helsinki and Turku)
DESCRIPTION:A new pilot residency from Art and Media Culture Agency M-Cult 
 in Helsinki and Arte artists' association / Titanik gallery in Turku\, sit
 ting at the intersection of art\, media and sonic practice\, funded by the
  Nordic-Baltic Mobility Programme for Culture from Nordic Culture Point. T
 WO ARTISTS will be selected. THE PREMISE: the programme treats technology 
 as a situated practice and deliberately shifts attention away from the pro
 gress narratives promoted by large global technology companies\, toward lo
 cal\, indigenous and community-oriented perspectives. It focuses on making
  vernacular and non-linear technological and sonic environments visible\, 
 foregrounding vibrant local histories and traditions across the Nordic and
  Baltic region\, and asks what non-linear sonic and media practice might l
 ook like in the current social\, political and cultural landscape. WHAT EA
 CH ARTIST RECEIVES: EUR 3\,500 artist fee\; EUR 2\,000 production budget\;
  accommodation for two months divided between Helsinki and Turku\, one mon
 th in each\; travel costs for one return ticket from a Nordic-Baltic count
 ry\; access to Titanik's residency studio space and a working desk at M-Cu
 lt's office during each stay\; mentoring and curatorial support from both 
 organisations' staff\; and networking into the Finnish media art and conte
 mporary art scenes. In return the artist develops in-depth or focused rese
 arch and presents their practice and research at two public events\, one i
 n each city. WHO CAN APPLY: individual artists only\, explicitly NOT colle
 ctives this round\; artists from or based in Finland\, Sweden\, Norway\, D
 enmark\, Iceland\, Aland\, the Faroe Islands\, Greenland\, Estonia\, Latvi
 a or Lithuania\; working in sound\, media and/or digital arts\, or the exp
 anded field of vernacular technologies\, all defined broadly by the organi
 sers. Applicants must be available to travel to Finland for the full two-m
 onth period across February and March 2027. The organisers welcome applica
 nts at all career stages and from all backgrounds. One submission per arti
 st: if two are sent\, only the later one is reviewed. HOW TO APPLY: submit
  via the form linked from the open call page with a brief letter of motiva
 tion including a research plan for the residency\, how it connects with th
 e programme's themes and what you would focus on (max 1-2 pages)\, your CV
 \, and a link to your portfolio. Applications in English only. DEADLINE: 3
 0 August 2026 at 23:59 UTC+3. Online interviews with shortlisted artists t
 ake place before 19 September 2026\, and all applicants are notified by em
 ail by the end of September 2026. JURY: Jussi Koitela\, Director of M-Cult
 \, Helsinki\, and Farbod Fakharzadeh\, Director of Arte ry and Titanik\, T
 urku. ACCOMMODATION AND ACCESSIBILITY: in Helsinki\, the Vallila Residency
  hosted by M-Cult is a 30 sqm apartment in a small wooden house with one r
 oom\, kitchen and bathroom with shower\; it is NOT fully accessible\, sitt
 ing on the first floor up stairs with no elevator (details from info@m-cul
 t.org). In Turku\, the Titanik A.i.R. apartment is 27 sqm on the top floor
  of a building with an elevator\, but the space including the bathroom is 
 not fully accessible (details from info@titanik.fi). A partner\, friend or
  child may join\, but their expenses are not covered. THE PARTNERS: M-Cult
 \, founded in 2000\, develops and presents art and media culture with a fo
 cus on the social and political dimensions of media and technology\, worki
 ng through art commissions\, residencies and learning programmes. Titanik\
 , established in Turku in 1988 by the artist-run organization Arte Ry\, ha
 s since 2006 run Titanik A.i.R.\, the first artist residency in Finland wi
 th a focus on sound art and sonic practices. CONTACT: Jussi Koitela (jussi
 .koitela@m-cult.org) or Farbod Fakharzadeh (director@arte.fi).\n\nOrganisa
 tion: Art and Media Culture Agency M-Cult (Helsinki) and Arte artists' ass
 ociation / Titanik gallery (Turku)\nLocation: Finland\; one month in Helsi
 nki and one month in Turku. Open to artists from or based in Nordic and Ba
 ltic countries.\nAward: EUR 3\,500 artist fee plus EUR 2\,000 production b
 udget\, two months' accommodation split between Helsinki and Turku\, and o
 ne return travel ticket from a Nordic-Baltic country. No application fee s
 tated.\nDuration: Two months\, February to March 2027 (one month in each c
 ity)\n\nMore: https://www.titanik.fi/residency-open-call-residing-non-line
 ar-technologies-and-sonic-worldings/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk
 &utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.titanik.fi/residency-open-call-residing-non-linear-technolo
 gies-and-sonic-worldings/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium
 =referral
LOCATION:Finland\; one month in Helsinki and one month in Turku. Open to ar
 tists from or based in Nordic and Baltic countries.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:normsy-social-media-measurement-methods-incubator-2026@artificialnouvea
 u.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261025T090229Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:Deadline: Normsy: Social Media Measurement Methods Incubator RFP
DESCRIPTION:Normsy\, a project of Civic Health Project\, runs a small metho
 ds incubator for new strategies to measure or estimate the impact of proso
 cial social-media interventions. Normsy identifies toxic posts (typically 
 content attacking or violating civic norms) and offers real users a set of
  AI-generated prosocial comments to choose from and post\; the open questi
 on is how to estimate the effect of those comments on content producers\, 
 other commenters and bystanders who consume content without interacting me
 asurably. The current approach combines survey experiments (how active X u
 sers respond emotionally to threads with and without the intervention\, an
 d how it shifts agreement with and support for the original post) with pai
 d X API platform data (classifying comments before and after an interventi
 on\, plus coarse engagement metrics that cost money each time they are rec
 ollected). WHAT THEY WANT: a systematic way to leverage available platform
  data and maximise the utility of the survey experiments. Example proposal
 s include a strategy for measuring toxicity/prosociality and reach/visibil
 ity of a thread over time with a randomisation design generating a control
  group of threads\, or an approach to benchmarking the prosocial potential
  of AI-generated comments through an iterative arena with human judges or 
 a theory-backed LLM validation approach with human validation. The brief i
 s explicit that methodological innovation\, causal identification\, scalab
 le measurement frameworks and novel observational approaches matter far mo
 re than proposing new intervention types\; proposals need not study Normsy
  exclusively but must explain how the evaluation design transfers to it. E
 thics and IRB considerations are central and Normsy will collaborate with 
 selected researchers on ethics review and implementation planning. Researc
 hers retain publication independence and IP\; the partnership is expected 
 to produce data for an academic paper. SUBMIT: one zipped folder through t
 he linked Google Form containing a 3-page proposal (design\, work already 
 done\, work still needed to scale and implement)\, a budget document for t
 he USD 20\,000\, a timeline with concrete deliverables inside 6 months\, a
 nd short bios for all team members. Award notifications mid-October 2026. 
 Questions to kylan@civichealthproject.org\; informal and exploratory conve
 rsations welcome.\n\nOrganisation: Normsy (a project of Civic Health Proje
 ct)\nLocation: Remote. Open to individuals and teams from academic\, acade
 mic-adjacent and industry backgrounds\; no stated country restriction. Res
 earch context is X/Twitter (proposals may be multi-platform but must inclu
 de X).\nAward: USD 1\,000 awarded to each of the top 5 proposals\, plus up
  to USD 20\,000 to fund R&D and/or testing with one selected partner indiv
 idual or team. Preference for budgets where at least 50 percent goes to re
 search costs (survey respondents\, LLM or X API credits). No application f
 ee.\nDuration: Proposals must set out concrete deliverables within 6 month
 s of starting\; the partnership is framed as the start of a potentially lo
 nger-term collaboration\n\nMore: https://normsy.ai/labs/openrfp?utm_source
 =artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://normsy.ai/labs/openrfp?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&ut
 m_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote. Open to individuals and teams from academic\, academic-adj
 acent and industry backgrounds\; no stated country restriction. Research c
 ontext is X/Twitter (proposals may be multi-platform but must include X).
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bold-lab-bold-fellow-oxford-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261025T090229Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260915
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260916
SUMMARY:Deadline: BOLD Fellow\, British Open-ended Learning and Discovery L
 ab
DESCRIPTION:The British Open-ended Learning and Discovery (BOLD) Lab is a n
 ew UKRI-funded initiative establishing an AI research laboratory led from 
 the University of Oxford\, bringing more than 80 researchers from Oxford\,
  UCL and Imperial College London under one vision. It carries an initial i
 nvestment of around GBP 8 million with the potential for a further GBP 22 
 million extending the investment over six years\, making it one of the UK'
 s largest bets on fundamental AI research. The lab's remit is ambitious\, 
 high-risk work on the fundamental science of artificial intelligence\, alo
 ngside training future leaders in the field. THE ROLE: up to 12 BOLD Fello
 ws are being recruited across the three universities. Fellows are expected
  to operate with a high level of independence and responsibility\, setting
  their own research direction\, publishing at the highest level\, and ment
 oring junior researchers and doctoral students. They also play a leading r
 ole in managing the lab and take responsibility for developing its strateg
 ic and scientific direction\, with primary responsibility for a programme 
 of independent funded research projects. The posting frames the draw as ex
 ceptional independence\, direct collaboration with world-leading colleague
 s across the three institutions\, access to substantial and rapidly growin
 g compute\, and real influence over the intellectual direction and culture
  of a major national lab. WHO SHOULD APPLY: senior researchers with an est
 ablished\, original research track record in AI or a closely related field
 \, who are a recognised authority in their area. HOW TO APPLY: submit an o
 nline application with a covering letter or supporting statement\, a CV\, 
 and details of two referees. Informal enquiries are welcomed beforehand to
  the BOLD Exec Team at boldexec-team@eng.ox.ac.uk. NOTE: this is a salarie
 d fixed-term research post rather than a grant. Only applications received
  before midday on Tuesday 15 September 2026 can be considered.\n\nOrganisa
 tion: University of Oxford\, Department of Engineering Science (BOLD Lab\,
  with UCL and Imperial College London)\nLocation: Oxford\, UK\, with posts
  also based at UCL and Imperial College London. The 12 posts are employed 
 across the three universities.\nAward: Salary on Oxford Grade 10R\, GBP 65
 \,336 to GBP 84\,663 per year\, with the posting noting that further salar
 y options are being explored. Benefits include associate membership at Reu
 ben College\, 38 days annual leave\, pension scheme\, childcare services\,
  and travel expenses for stakeholder and conference visits. Fellows also g
 et access to substantial compute: 3 million GPU hours on Isambard for the 
 first 18 months\, with the lab working towards 5\,000 H100-equivalents in 
 total across industry and government support. No application fee.\nDuratio
 n: Fixed-term\, 12 months\, funded by UKRI\n\nMore: https://eng.ox.ac.uk/j
 obs/job-detail?vacancyID=187853&utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_
 medium=referral
URL:https://eng.ox.ac.uk/jobs/job-detail?vacancyID=187853&utm_source=artifi
 cialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Oxford\, UK\, with posts also based at UCL and Imperial College Lo
 ndon. The 12 posts are employed across the three universities.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:new-public-entrepreneur-in-residence-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261025T090229Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260909
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260910
SUMMARY:Deadline: New_ Public Entrepreneur in Residence\, Pilot Cohort
DESCRIPTION:New_ Public is piloting a virtual Entrepreneur in Residence pro
 gramme for solo founders building alternatives to the mainstream social pl
 atforms. Its framing: people are turning away from algorithmic social medi
 a\, AI bots are taking over the big platforms\, and users are retreating i
 nto private closed spaces\, which leaves an opening for builders working o
 n a new information ecosystem rather than the status quo. The residency su
 pplies research\, experts\, connections and tools\; the founder brings the
  vision and experience. TWO FOCUS AREAS: humans connecting to humans\, mea
 ning products that bring people into meaningful contact online (forums\, m
 essaging apps\, groups\, microblogging\, in-person meetups)\, including AI
 -powered platforms and tools as long as they connect people to people rath
 er than people to bots\; and local and civic information environments\, me
 aning tools for distributing local news\, building community knowledge and
  facilitating collective sensemaking\, so people can get trustworthy infor
 mation about their communities. WHAT RESIDENTS DO: use self-directed time 
 to prototype ideas\, test assumptions and gain clarity\; participate in re
 gular check-ins\, engage with the other resident and the wider team\, and 
 take candid feedback on product and strategy\; engage seriously with go-to
 -market questions about who the user is\, how they find you\, and the path
  to sustainability\; and help shape the programme itself\, since the pilot
  cohort's feedback directly informs how the EIR programme runs going forwa
 rd. WHO THEY WANT: entrepreneurial founders ready to build their next thin
 g within the focus areas\, with a track record of or clear drive toward wo
 rk that builds social trust\, connection and belonging\; strong product th
 inkers with good judgment\, a relentless focus on user needs\, and opennes
 s to coaching on mission-aligned business models. An early idea is enough\
 , no working product required. The pilot is open to founders with somethin
 g else going on alongside\, though the full programme will most likely be 
 full time. APPLICATION PROCESS: submit the written application online\; sh
 ortlisted applicants submit a short video by 18 September 2026 covering ba
 ckground\, motivation and a quick pitch\; full applications and videos go 
 to the advisory board\, with offers made in early October ahead of the kic
 koff. FUTURE COHORTS: New_ Public plans to open the next cohort to the UK 
 and EU from spring 2027. Deadline 11:59pm Pacific on Wednesday 9 September
  2026.\n\nOrganisation: New_ Public\nLocation: Remote\, with an in-person 
 kickoff retreat in Philadelphia\, USA (19 to 22 October 2026). Open to app
 licants based in the United States or Canada.\nAward: Stipend of USD 22\,5
 00\, paid in three monthly installments across the residency\, each contin
 gent on continued active participation. Plus USD 1\,500 toward an AI tool 
 of the resident's choice for coding\, prototyping or design. Two residents
  are selected in the pilot cohort. Also provided: hands-on product and str
 ategy support from senior New_ Public staff and outside experts\; access t
 o New_ Public's five years of original research on moderation\, governance
 \, community stewardship and platform design\, plus direct access to the r
 esearchers behind it\; and introductions across New_ Public's network of m
 ajor platforms\, startups\, university researchers and funders for hiring\
 , fundraising\, early user testing and community partnerships. Residents m
 ust submit a Form W-9 or W-8BEN to receive the stipend. No application fee
 .\nDuration: Three months\, from the week of 19 October 2026 to 29 January
  2027\, run remotely apart from the opening retreat\n\nMore: https://newpu
 blic.org/EIR?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://newpublic.org/EIR?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_med
 ium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, with an in-person kickoff retreat in Philadelphia\, USA (
 19 to 22 October 2026). Open to applicants based in the United States or C
 anada.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bulletin-editorial-fellowship-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261025T090229Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260828
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260829
SUMMARY:Deadline: Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists Editorial Fellowship 20
 26
DESCRIPTION:The Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists runs an Editorial Fellows
  Program for emerging leaders who can give its audience regular and expans
 ive coverage of the Bulletin's core issues\, the existential threats facin
 g humanity. TRACKS: two specialty tracks are open in this round\, biosecur
 ity and disruptive technology/AI. Fellows write for the Bulletin's editori
 al pages\, producing up to four published pieces over the year at USD 750 
 each\, with limited travel costs and occasional incidental expenses covere
 d on approval. WHO SHOULD APPLY: this is not a student programme. Applican
 ts need significant professional experience\, an established record of pub
 lishing quality work\, and recognition as an emerging leader in their fiel
 d\; undergraduate and graduate students may apply only if they bring subst
 antial writing experience and published work. No geographic or citizenship
  restriction is stated. HOW TO APPLY: email a resume\, a cover letter (the
  call stresses that the cover letter matters)\, and up to three writing sa
 mples\, with 'editorial fellow' as the subject line\, to the address given
  on the call page. Fellows are announced in September 2026. Deadline 28 Au
 gust 2026.\n\nOrganisation: Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists\nLocation: R
 emote. No geographic or citizenship restriction is stated.\nAward: USD 750
  per published piece\, for up to four pieces annually\, so up to USD 3\,00
 0. Limited travel costs and occasional incidental expenses are also covere
 d. No application fee.\nDuration: Annual fellowship\, built around up to f
 our published pieces\n\nMore: https://thebulletin.org/2026/08/apply-to-be-
 a-2026-bulletin-editorial-fellow/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&ut
 m_medium=referral
URL:https://thebulletin.org/2026/08/apply-to-be-a-2026-bulletin-editorial-f
 ellow/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote. No geographic or citizenship restriction is stated.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:epic-megagrants-cycle-2-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261025T090229Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260904
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260905
SUMMARY:Deadline: Epic MegaGrants 2026 (Cycle 2)
DESCRIPTION:Epic MegaGrants are financial grants from Epic Games for creati
 ve\, noteworthy and innovative projects built in and around Unreal Engine\
 , or projects that enhance the open source 3D graphics ecosystem. Over 2\,
 000 grants have been awarded to date. The programme is explicitly aimed at
  smaller teams\, solo developers and individuals as well as studios: Epic 
 states that individuals and small teams are eligible and encouraged\, and 
 that it treats every project equally regardless of who you are. WHAT IS FU
 NDED: (1) INDIE GAMES\, small or solo teams working in Unreal Engine\, sub
 mitted past the idea and design phase with at least some working prototype
 \, ideally with a video link\; (2) UEFN EXPERIENCES\, Fortnite Creative is
 lands and interactive experiences built in Unreal Editor for Fortnite\; (3
 ) ENGINE MIGRATION\, a proportion of MegaGrants is reserved for teams movi
 ng a partially developed project from another engine to Unreal\; (4) FILM\
 , TV AND LIVE EVENTS\, including live-action and animated films and shows\
 , broadcast\, live and location-based events\; (5) OTHER NON-GAMES VERTICA
 LS such as architecture\, automotive\, manufacturing\, simulation\, produc
 t design\, advertising and VR/AR\, where more detailed project plans and p
 rototypes are expected\; (6) EDUCATION\, including research\, curricula an
 d course development and university programmes\, though for institutions E
 pic accepts submissions only from degree-granting schools and educators at
  such schools\; (7) TOOLS AND OPEN SOURCE\, creating open source tools or 
 enhancing open source software using Unreal Engine. NON-UNREAL PROJECTS AR
 E ELIGIBLE if you want to move them to Unreal Engine\, or if the project e
 nhances open source 3D content creation whether or not it relates to Unrea
 l Engine. EVALUATION rests on three things: quality and unique appeal (hig
 h-quality demos or builds\, well-documented descriptions\, technical ambit
 ion)\; community benefit (how you will give back\, for example open-sourci
 ng the project\, releasing assets through a marketplace\, sharing learning
 s\, or shipping an SDK\, tool or plug-in)\; and viability (a clear budget 
 with major spend areas and a project plan with milestones\, deliverables a
 nd timeline). NOTE ON DEADLINES: the FAQ still describes MegaGrants as an 
 ongoing programme with no specific deadline\, but the timeline published o
 n the same page now runs in fixed cycles\, and the Cycle 2 submission wind
 ow closes 4 September 2026 at 23:59 ET. Treat the cycle date as the operat
 ive deadline. You may apply for more than one project\, and may reapply fo
 r the same project after a decision if you have made updates. Epic is not 
 offering sponsorships and cannot give feedback on applications. Apply at m
 egagrants.unrealengine.com.\n\nOrganisation: Epic Games\nLocation: Open wo
 rldwide. Epic states that if it can legally make payment to you\, you are 
 eligible.\nAward: Typical grants range from USD 5\,000 to USD 75\,000\, wi
 th some exceptional projects receiving up to USD 150\,000. Epic states tha
 t recipients continue to own their IP and may publish it however they wish
 \, so this is a grant rather than an investment or equity deal. Grants up 
 to and including USD 25\,000 may be paid in a single instalment\; larger g
 rants are paid across several instalments tied to continued progress. Fund
 s are for project and team expenses such as development and hiring\; hardw
 are purchases or rentals are approved only occasionally\, case by case. No
  application fee.\nDuration: Cycle 2 of 2026 runs 29 June to 11 December 2
 026. Submission window 29 June to 4 September 2026 (closes 23:59 ET)\, rev
 iew 5 September to 4 December 2026\, notices 7 to 11 December 2026. Cycle 
 1 ran 12 January to 19 June 2026.\n\nMore: https://www.unrealengine.com/me
 gagrants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.unrealengine.com/megagrants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugra
 ntdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Open worldwide. Epic states that if it can legally make payment to
  you\, you are eligible.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:futures-of-listening-architecture-on-sound-2027@artificialnouveau.githu
 b.io
DTSTAMP:20261025T090229Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260913
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260914
SUMMARY:Deadline: Futures of Listening: Architecture on Sound Residency
DESCRIPTION:One residency for an individual practitioner\, a duo or a studi
 o working at the intersection of sound and space\, exploring architecture 
 through spatial audio. THE PREMISE: architecture is traditionally conceive
 d through form\, structure\, material and light\, with sound treated as so
 mething to be controlled or optimised afterwards. This residency asks what
  happens if architecture begins with sound\, shifting the question from ho
 w a space should sound to how sound can generate space. Spatial audio\, vi
 rtual acoustics and immersive media now allow prototyping of responsive en
 vironments\, impossible architectures and sonic spaces beyond physical con
 straints. RESEARCH QUESTIONS include how sound can become a first principl
 e in architectural and spatial design\, how the acoustic properties of spa
 ce inform the creation and perception of spatial sound\, and how artistic 
 research can shape the future tools\, interfaces and methods of spatial so
 und creation. NOT JUST ARTWORKS: they explicitly say they want new methods
 \, insights and creative infrastructures too. Outcomes may include install
 ations\, spatial audio compositions\, performances\, audiovisual works\, s
 oftware prototypes\, reusable methodologies or modular interfaces. Residen
 ts may collaborate with a 4DSOUND software developer to prototype new tool
 s\, and practitioners who already build their own software\, hardware or c
 reative technologies are encouraged to advance those as part of the reside
 ncy. WHO: architects are the named invitees\, with scenographers\, spatial
  designers\, installation artists\, sound artists\, composers and creative
  technologists also encouraged. Creative coding experience is welcome but 
 not required. Lead researcher is Poul Holleman\, co-founder and director o
 f 4DSOUND. HARD ELIGIBILITY GATE: applicants must be based in the Netherla
 nds AND registered as an artist with the Dutch Chamber of Commerce (KvK). 
 This is not negotiable and rules out non-NL applicants. Futures of Listeni
 ng is a Netherlands-based platform for spatial sound\, immersive experienc
 e\, performance and installation art\, founded by FIBER Festival\, 4DSOUND
 \, Robin Koek and Art of Sound at the Royal Conservatoire The Hague\, supp
 orted by CIIIC and Stimuleringsfonds.\n\nOrganisation: Futures of Listenin
 g (FIBER\, 4DSOUND\, Robin Koek\, Art of Sound)\nLocation: Amsterdam\, Net
 herlands\, with work at the 4DSOUND studio.\nAward: Total residency fee of
  EUR 7\,125\, calculated as 25 commissioned days at a day rate of EUR 285.
  Six of those 25 days can be spent at the 4DSOUND studio working with thei
 r team. Some additional financial support is available if needed\, includi
 ng material and travel costs\, but these must be clearly defined and agree
 d before the residency starts. No application fee.\nDuration: December 202
 6 to February 2027\, structured as 25 commissioned days. Deadline 13 Septe
 mber 2026.\n\nMore: https://fiber.homerun.co/artist-residency-architecture
 -on-sound/en?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://fiber.homerun.co/artist-residency-architecture-on-sound/en?utm_
 source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Amsterdam\, Netherlands\, with work at the 4DSOUND studio.
END:VEVENT
